  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 AM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nToday through Tue night...Closed upper level low will move \nslowly through the ID panhandle today and reach north central MT\nby Mon morning. This far south, only the extreme northern end \nof the forecast area has any sort of risk for precipitation, and\nnear zero chance of thunderstorms. Once the low passes to the\neast, dry westerly flow fills in behind it. A second low digs\nalong the US West coast, arriving at the OR-CA border at the end\nof this period. This allows a weak ridge over the forecast area\nwith a shift to southwest flow. This will mean nearly clear\nskies and zero chance of precipitation, with warming starting\nTue and continuing Wed. \n\nThe first low has a strong jet streak on the southern side of\nthe low that moves through the forecast area, supporting much\nstronger wind at the surface. The wind is strong enough on the\nnorthwest side of the Snake River plain to trigger a Wind\nAdvisory from late this morning to early evening. After this,\nthe gradient relaxes overnight and do not expect a repeat on\nMon. \n\nWed through Sat...The upper level low at the coast starts moving\ninland, bring a chance for precipitation and thunderstorms on\nWed night through Thu night. This low brings another wind event\non Thu and Thu night. Another high moves in behind and keeps \nconditions dry and warm through the end of the period.\nTemperatures stay above normal through this period.",
